/id #edb/ref 17592186148603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147622 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147304 :question/id #uuid "80466688-43e1-5697-976c-6ba63844fed9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2780"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947eb2bb-0443-5740-9d45-847c70877270" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567037}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given Some Lengths"} :step/id #uuid "f37e8c00-7648-50da-a5ea-514aa04c1594" :step/math-academy-id 9294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148726 :tutorial/content "The **surface area** of a polyhedron is the combined area of all its faces. \n\nTo calculate the surface area of a polyhedron, we use nets. \n\nLet's consider the net of the rectangular pyramid below. What is the surface area of this pyramid?\n\n![](images/7f/7f1843bc6f3c0e331b99045c731368771f831abdccdb7c255606d39a0fb616e4.png)\n\nThe surface area of our pyramid consists of one rectangle (the base) and four triangles (the side faces). \n\nAlso, notice that there are two different triangular faces:\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $60\\,\\text{in}^2,$ and\n\n- there are two triangular faces with an area of $30\\,\\text{in}^2.$\n\nCalculating the total area, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times 120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+2\\times 60\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+2\\times 30\\,{\\text{in}}^{2} \\\\  & =120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+120\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}+60\\,{\\text{in}}^{2} \\\\  & =300\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the surface area of the pyramid is $300 \\, \\text{in}^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2c9b151-2ae3-545d-aecf-0d7faa137775" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2329 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "eb350109-9beb-546a-a0c5-41e6f139bab5" :step/math-academy-id 9709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148598}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148633 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148727 :tutorial/content "If we know a polyhedron's measurements, we can calculate its surface area by first drawing the net of the polyhedron. Then, we calculate the areas of its polygonal faces using known formulas and then sum the results. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the surface area of the rectangular prism shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85ddfbae506653a6b2c4982be74f9eff2b91df55a5075597ddbbd42a51cc2f52.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we consider the net of our prism.\n\n![](images/98/9874d746b2510403accdbd2e9d77ce8d5bc917b76e68c3f1690a24e8643ad473.png)\n\n      \n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the prism is made up of squares and rectangles.\n\n- The rectangles are $8\\,\\text{ft}$ long and $2\\,\\text{ft}$ high. So, the area of one rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of rectangle} = 8 \\times 2 = 16 \\, \\text{ft}^2.\n$$\n\n- The squares have sides of length $2\\, \\text{ft}.$ So, the area of one square is\n\n$$\n\\text{Area of square} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\, \\text{ft}^2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the surface area of the prism consists of $4$ rectangles and $2$ squares.\n\nAdding the areas, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of rectangle})+2\\times (\\text{Area of square}) \\\\  & =4\\times 16+2\\times 4 \\\\  & =64+8 \\\\  & =72\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c41c924e-2d7e-5137-a601-92722cbb069a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2330 :tutorial/title "Finding Surface Areas of Polyhedra Given Some Lengths"} :step/id #uuid "cf84acc6-2c16-5427-b489-739a60c4925d" :step/math-academy-id 9710 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148603}}] :activity/title "Finding Surface Areas Using Nets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147669}]}
